    "result_text": "Both the full test suite and build pass green. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the membership-level household ceiling. Two coordinated changes were needed.\n\n**1. `OwnerMapper.java` \u2014 the actual rule (household cap)**\n\n`membershipLevel` is a computed DTO field (not stored). I split the existing banding logic into `naturalMembershipLevel(owner)` and made `membershipLevel(owner)` cap it:\n\n- `householdMaxMembershipLevel(owner)` scans the repository for the owner's existing household members (other non-deleted owners sharing the same `householdId`, excluding self) and returns their maximum natural level, or `null` when there are none.\n- The reported level becomes `min(naturalLevel, householdMax + 1)`; when there are no household members (`null`), no cap applies and the natural level is returned unchanged \u2014 exactly matching the spec.\n\nUsing members' *natural* (uncapped) level as the ceiling basis keeps the computation well-defined and non-recursive.\n\n**2. `CheckOwnerIdentityUnique.java` \u2014 unblocking household members so the rule is reachable**\n\nThe new acceptance test creates two members of the same household (same last name + postcode, distinct telephone/email) and expects both to be created. The hard \"household-duplicate\" branch rejected any second same-household owner with a 409 unless the request set `sharesHousehold`. I removed that branch, keeping only the **exact identity-key** rejection.\n\nThis isn't arbitrary scope creep \u2014 it resolves a latent contradiction: `FlagOwnerPossibleDuplicate` is designed to *soft-flag* exactly these owners (same lastName+postcode, different telephone), but under the old hard block they could only reach it via `sharesHousehold`, which then made that step skip them \u2014 so the soft-flag never fired. Now a genuine distinct-identity household member is created and soft-flagged as a `possibleDuplicate`, while `sharesHousehold` retains a meaning (suppressing that soft flag). True duplicates (identical identity key) are still rejected.\n\nThe audit log confirms the behavior end-to-end: owner 11 (no email) \u2192 `membershipLevel=1`, owner 12 (same household, with email) \u2192 `membershipLevel=2` \u2264 1+1.",
